# Build scratch, normally cleaned up.
.test.log
checker/dist/.build.js
consumer/deno.lock
*.coverprofile
coverage.out
cover.out

# checker/dist/ and fixtures/ are generated but committed: make check rebuilds
# both and fails if the tree is stale.

# Derived conformance corpus: 640 files the Python oracle writes and Go and the TypeScript
# checker read to prove all three agree. `make` regenerates it from `tools/make_ir_cases.py`
# (the `generated` target), so it is reproducible from source and does not belong in history.
fixtures/generated/

# Derived the same way, by tools/make_refusals.py: one proposal per refusal code, rewritten
# from scratch on every run. The `refusalfixtures` target produces them. What is asserted is
# the CODE each one raises, not its bytes, so the files themselves carry nothing git needs.
fixtures/refusals/

# Derived the same way, by tools/make_corruptions.py: one good bundle per shape and one
# corruption per refusal class, rewritten from scratch on every run. What is asserted is
# the CLASS each one is refused with, not its bytes.
fixtures/bundles/

# Derived the same way, by tools/make_rejected.py: one document per thing a schema must
# refuse. What is asserted is that the Python validator AND the Go decoder both refuse each
# one -- and that Go has a decoder for every schema present, which is what makes a new
# schema land in both implementations or not at all.
fixtures/rejected/

# Derived the same way, by tools/make_synthesis.py: the oracle's own synthesis answers and
# one corruption per refusal class. What is asserted is that the TypeScript checker accepts
# each good answer and refuses each corruption with exactly the class it names.
fixtures/synthesis/
